'''terrosity†''', ''n.'' [< *''terrous'' (< F. ''terreux'' = Pr. ''terros'', < L. ''terrosus'', full of earth, earthy, < ''terra'', earth: see ''terra'', and cf. ''terreous'') + ''-ity''.] Earthiness.

{{smaller block|Rhenish wine . . . hath fewer dregs and less ''terresity'' [read ''terrosity''] or gross earthliness than the Clared wine hath. {{right|''W. Turner'' (Arber's Eng. Garner, II. 114).}}}}

'''terry''' (ter′i), ''n.'' [Origin obscure.] '''1.''' A textile fabric of wool or silk, woven like velvet, but with the loops uncut.

{{smaller block|The furniture was in green ''terry'', the carpet a harsh, brilliant tapestry. {{right|''Howells'', Annie Kilburn, xi.}}}}

'''2.''' In ''rope-making'', an open reel. ''E. H. Knight.'' {{smaller|—'''Terry poplin.''' See ''poplin''.—'''Terry velvet''', uncut velvet.}}

'''Tersanctus''' (tėr′sangk″tus), ''n.'' [< L. ''ter'', thrice (see ''ter''), + ''sanctus'', holy (see ''saint''): so called because it begins with the word ''Sanctus'', said thrice.] Same as ''Sanctus''.

'''terse<sup>1</sup>''' (tėrs), ''a.'' [= Sp. Pg. It. ''terso'', < L. ''tersus'', wiped off, clean, neat, pure, pp. of ''tergere'', wipe, rub off, wipe dry, polish.] '''1†.''' Wiped; rubbed; appearing as if wiped or rubbed; smooth.

{{smaller block|Many stones also, both precious and vulgar, although ''terse'' and smooth, have not this power attractive. {{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., ii. 4.}}}}

'''2†.''' Refined; accomplished; polished: said of persons.

{{smaller block|Your polite and ''terse'' gallants. {{right|''Massinger.''}}}}

'''3.''' Free from superfluity; neatly or elegantly compact or concise; neat; concise.

{{smaller block|In eight ''terse'' lines has Phædrus told
(So frugal were the bards of old)}}

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|A tale of goats; and clos'd with grace
Plan, moral, all, in that short space.
{{right|''W. Whitehead'', The Goat's Beard.}}}}

¶
'''terse<sup>2</sup>†''', ''n.'' See ''terce.''

'''tersely''' (tėrs′li), ''adv.'' '''1†.''' In an accomplished manner.

{{smaller block|Fastidious Brisk, a neat, spruce, affecting courtier, . . . speaks good remnants; . . . swears ''tersely'' and with variety. {{right|''B. Jonson'', Every Man out of his Humour.}}}}

'''2.''' In a terse manner; neatly; compactly; concisely.

'''terseness''' (tėrs′nes), ''n.'' '''1.''' The state or property of being terse; neatness of style; compactness; conciseness; brevity.

{{smaller block|Under George the First, the monotonous smoothness of Byron's versification and the ''terseness'' of his expression would have made Pope himself envious.
{{right|''Macaulay'', Moore's Byron.}}}}

'''2.''' Shortness. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The cylindrical figure of the mole, as well as the compactness of its form, arising from the ''terseness'' of its limbs, proportionally lessens its labour.
{{right|''Paley'', Nat. Theol., xv.}}}}

'''tersion''' (tėr′shọn), ''n.'' [< L. ''tergere'', pp. ''tersus'', wipe.] The act of wiping or rubbing; friction; cleaning.

{{smaller block|He [Boyle] found also that heat and ''tersion'' (or the cleaning or wiping of any body) increased its susceptibility of [electric] excitation. {{right|''Encyc. Brit.'', VIII. 3.}}}}

